Sval: page rules from the content classifier's engines

adblock-rust already computes a page's hiding selectors, procedural actions
and scriptlets (Engine::url_cosmetic_resources, hidden_class_id_selectors),
but the engine crate's FFI exposes network matching only and the engines live
inside ContentClassifierService. This exposes them, so one engine and one set
of lists serve requests and pages:
nsIContentClassifierService gains pageRules, the answers of the engines that
block in the window's kind merged into one nsIContentClassifierPageRules and
cached by address until those engines change, so a page's frames read one
answer; hiddenClassIdSelectors; and setScriptletResources, the scriptlet
library, parsed and stored once and shared by every engine, so installing it
in an engine under the service's lock copies nothing. The FFI is Sval's
page_rules.rs, a module of the engine crate, and the C++ around it is
SvalClassifierPageRules, which ContentClassifierEngine names its friend.

The engines are private to ContentClassifierService and the FFI is compiled
Rust, so no chrome API reaches them.

